feat: ADFGX / ADFGVX ciphers, wired into the toolkit and the analyzer

Requested because the classical collection had playfair, hill, columnar, rail fence and
a dozen more, but not these two. New module utils/adfgvx.py (delegated implementation;
wired, reviewed and integrated here):

- adfgx_square / adfgvx_square: keyword squares, ADFGX folds J onto I, ADFGVX is A-Z0-9
- adfgx_encrypt/decrypt, adfgvx_encrypt/decrypt, with an explicit digit_substitution for
  the 6x6 square (a keyword with too few unique letters raises instead of being padded)
- adfgvx_detect: a stream made only of the ADFGX/ADFGVX labels is a strong signature, the
  verdict carries the per-label counts as evidence, and an odd-length ADFGVX stream is
  reported as un-pairable rather than accepted
- adfgvx_crack: exhaustive read orders up to 6!/7!/8! by effort, simulated annealing
  above; every reported plaintext is re-encrypted and must reproduce the ciphertext.
  Without a square it only answers when word evidence passes a measured gate, otherwise
  ok=False with the best round trip kept as a hypothesis - the implementer added that gate
  after measuring that a joint grid fit drove the score to ~115 on pure noise
- package-root exports plus __all__, and analyze now reports an ADFGX/ADFGVX detection
  with its evidence instead of scoring a coordinate stream as if it were English
- tests/test_adfgvx.py: the hand-checkable ZEBRA/KEY vector (ATTACK -> XGXFAXAAGXDD),
  ragged-grid round trips, digit substitution and its refusals, detection against
  plaintext/base64/odd-length/junk, the known-square crack proved by re-encryption, the
  honest no-square refusal, and a static check that no score_text call in the search
  objective takes a flag bonus (house rule: a flag bonus lets hill climbing invent flags)

Measured by the implementer: known-square cracks are sub-second at k<=6, k=8 exhaustive
takes about 15s, k=9 is solved at effort=deep but not at normal, and k=11/12 were not
recovered; without a square only a keyword present in the guess list is found. Both limits
are documented in the module docstring.

Tests: 218, all green.
